// case: poisoned-annotation-is-refused
// what: `poisoned` was the spelling the checker wrote into a symbol whose
//      declaration it had refused, so a program must not be able to declare a
//      name with it. checkAnnotation guards capitalised names only and
//      checkCompatibility accepted the spelling for any expected type, so
//      `val p: poisoned = 7` checked clean and the C emitter gave `p` the type
//      it gives every name it has no rule for. The sentinel reads `<poisoned>`
//      now, which parseTypeName cannot produce, so this annotation is an
//      ordinary unknown type again and the value is refused against it.
// expect-error: 'p' is declared poisoned but the value is int.
fn main() -> int {
    val p: poisoned = 7
    return p
}
